\begin_layout Standard
Efficient models are key to maximizing the lifespan and performance of lithium-ion battery (LIB) cells in electric vehicle (EV) applications.
 Lightweight,
 high-accuracy battery models enable fast-charging strategies and real-time controls that limit aging processes.
 While equivalent-circuit models (ECMs) have long been used for this purpose,
 physics-based models (PBMs) capable of predicting internal electrochemical variables offer substantial performance gains and hold promise for use in next-generation optimal battery control systems 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Zou-SPM-MPC-2018,MadduxFastCharge,borah2024synergizing,HahnRuther-MPC-TLM2021,Suresh-SPM-MPC-2018"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Yet,
 a lack of PBMs that are simultaneously practical for deployment in EVs and nearly as accurate as full-order partial-differential and algebraic equation (PDAE) models has stifled widespread adoption of PBM-based controls by the industry.
 A PBM of complexity comparable to ECMs with the accuracy of a PDAE model could bring next-generation battery controls to fruition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The full-order PDAE model introduced by Newman 
\emph on
et al
\emph default
.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
