).
 Among these,
 single-particle models (SPMs) are ubiquitous for their simplicity 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Suresh-SPM-MPC-2018,ChambersSPMs,Li_LIB_SPMe_2017,Zou-SPM-MPC-2018"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The primary limitation of SPMs is significant loss of accuracy at high C-rate—
especially for cells with thick electrodes employed in energy-dense applications 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "danner2016thick,khan2024computational"
literal "false"

\end_inset

—
stemming from the assumption that electrochemical variables remain uniform across the thickness of the porous electrodes.
 In particular,
 the lack of spatial resolution of overpotential in graphite electrodes impedes development of next-generation fast-charge controls designed to prevent lithium plating 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "HahnRuther-MPC-TLM2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Other ROMs remain accurate at high C-rate,
 but suffer from a similar lack of spatial resolution,
 including the tanks-in-series model developed by Subramaniam 
\emph on
et al
\emph default
.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "subramaniam2020efficient,SubramaniamTanksInSeriesLIB2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and the multi-particle model introduced by Majdabadi 
\emph on
et al
\emph default
.
