 and the transfer-function model given in ref.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "plett2024battery"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 offer spatial resolution of variables,
 but remain too complex to deploy in many practical EV applications.
 Baker and Verbrugge
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "VerbruggeSinusoidalCurrentScannning,VerbruggeROMPerturbation"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 introduce a 1-state perturbation model with a degree of spatial resolution and remarkable accuracy in low-frequency steady-state conditions;
 however,
 the model breaks down when the current applied to a cell changes suddenly.
 In ref.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "hileman2024physics"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we show that this perturbation model coincides with the low-frequency limit of the transfer function model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this article,
 we formulate a novel SPM with spatial resolution across the thickness of porous electrodes and accuracy at higher C-rates.
 Our spatially-resolved SPMe (SPMe+) fuses an enhanced SPM (SPMe) with linear time-variant filters and dc gains from the perturbation model to achieve spatial resolution of lithium concentration,
 reaction current and overpotential.
 For simplicity,
 we construct an SPMe+ for a lithium-ion half cell—
which happens to be useful for modeling lithium-metal battery cells as well—
but results extend easily to full LIB cells.
 In a simulation study,
 the SPMe+ predicts the voltage of an LMO half-cell with a 
\begin_inset Formula $\unit[200]{\mu m}$
\end_inset

 thick electrode at 
\begin_inset Formula $\unit[1.8]{C}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\unit[67]{\%}$
\end_inset

 less error than a vanilla SPMe,
 yet contributes only two additional states per electrode.
 
